there are three politicians that are now  calling for the current administration  to not  reappoint jerome powell as a federal  reserve chair what does that mean the  federal reserve chair is not an elected  official it's an appointed official so  we elect politicians into positions of  power they then have the opportunity for  certain  places of influence in the government uh  or in organizations to appoint people to  those seats so we don't get to elect  everyone but we do get elect uh the  politicians and so one of those seats is  obviously the chairman of the federal  reserve jerome powell was appointed back  in 2017 by president donald trump uh and  it is a four-year appointment so when  you get appointed basically you're  signing up for a four-year uh kind of  job stint and then at the end of your  job stint of the first four years uh you  can get reappointed if the president is  the same and you've done a good job  you're likely to get reappointed  sometimes if you've done a good job and  the presidential administration has  changed you may not get reappointed and  then obviously if you did a bad job  in the eyes of those making the  appointment decisions you could also get  fired as well  so  when you think about this it is normal  for this conversation to come up when we  get towards the end so 2017 we're in  2021 now um the term actually expires in  february uh of of this upcoming year and  so far president joe biden uh his white  house or his administration has not said  whether they are going to reappoint uh  jerome powell or not now i want to read  uh just a sentence or two from the full  statement from these three politicians  one is uh aoc uh the second is rashida  toleb i believe i pronounce it and  ayanna presley are the three politicians  and it says quote as news of the  possible reappointment of federal  reserve chair jerome powell circulates  we urge president biden to reimagine a  federal reserve focused on eliminating  climate risk and advancing racial and  economic justice now when you think  about this  it is pretty interesting because what  they're talking about explicitly is  eliminating climate risk and advancing  racial and economic justice  and so when you  ask yourself well what is the job of the  federal reserve right what is that job  uh we can go to federalreserve.gov  and this is exactly i'm just going to  read you word to word what they say what  is the purpose of the federal reserve  system the federal reserve system often  referred to as the federal reserve  simply the fed is the central bank of  the united states it was created by the  congress to provide the nation with a  safer more flexible and more stable  monetary and financial system the  federal reserve was created on december  23 1913 when president woodrow wilson  signed the federal reserve act into law  today the federal reserve's  responsibilities fall into four general  areas those four areas are the following  one conducting the nation's monetary  policy by influencing money money and  credit conditions in the economy in  pursuit of full employment and stable  prices first category of the federal  reserve's role is to pursue full  employment and stable prices  second supervising and regulating banks  and other important financial  institutions to ensure the safety and  soundness of the nation's banking and  financial system and to protect the  credit rights of consumers so the second  thing is to regulate the banks to create  that safety and soundness of the banking  and financial system the third thing is  maintaining the stability of the  financial system and containing systemic  risk that may arise in financial markets  so  systemic risk eliminate and contain uh  any of those risks the fourth thing  providing certain financial services to  the us government u.s financial  institutions and foreign official  institutions and playing a major role in  operating and overseeing the nation's  payment systems okay this is interesting  there's four things that the federal  reserve is focused on or four general  areas the first conducting the monetary  policy with the pursuit of full  employment and stable prices that's one  two is supervising and regulating banks  with a goal of ensuring safety and  soundness of the nation's banking and  financial system okay that sounds that  makes sense three maintaining the  stability of the financial system and  containing containing systemic risk  makes sense four providing certain  financial services to the us government  and other types of organizations  and operating and overseeing the  nation's payment systems got it all that  makes sense to me let's go back to the  statement from these three politicians  they want to reimagine a federal reserve  focused on eliminating climate risk and  advancing racial and economic justice  when you compare what these politicians  are asking versus the  directive the mandate of the federal  reserve they don't match now again  that's not to say that uh eliminating  climate risk and advancing racial and  economic justice is important or not  important it's just that's not the job  of the federal reserve the federal  reserve's job is in the four areas that  we just discussed and so i am incredibly  critical of the monetary policy  decisions i get made uh many times i'm  incredibly uh critical  in terms of the way that we are shared  information um in terms of inflation and  other monetary impact uh and also a  fiscal policy  but at the same time i think it's  important for  critics to also be intellectually honest  and say hey  there are things to critique but there's  also things that we must not uh allow to  enter into the conversation uh that  don't necessarily have anything to do  with what the federal reserve's mandate  is so we go and we actually look at the  four things that the federal reserve is  supposed to be focused on has nothing to  do with eliminating climate risk and has  nothing to do with advancing racial or  economic justice now you can argue that  there could be the impact of these  things but that is not ultimately the  mandate that we've given them one of the  questions could be maybe we should  change the federal reserve's mandate  that could be up for debate and if the  uh our elected officials and others in  appointed positions desire to change the  mandate of the federal reserve to  include these things that would be a  whole different conversation but as it  stands today the federal reserve is not  mandated with doing any of these things  instead it is focused on the four things  that we just described so if we go at  the bottom here there's one other  sentence i want to read which says to  move forward with a whole of government  approach that eliminates climate risk  while making our financial system safer  we need a chair who is committed to  these objectives and we urge the biden  administration to use the opportunity to  appoint a new federal reserve chair  now  there is the mention right there of the  financial system safer but  the idea that the federal reserve is  going to eliminate climate risk or  advance racial and economic justice does  not really coincide with the current  mandate so it brings up the question of  should we change the mandate or should  we simply say hey we have to  govern or we have to judge the success  of the federal reserve based on the four  criteria the four areas of focus now  there could be a strong argument to be  made that one of the things that the  government is supposed to be focused on  or the federal reserve is supposed to be  focused on is stable prices well we  don't have stable prices in america we  have cpi of over five percent so if we  don't have stable prices you could argue  that they are not doing a good job of  meeting that mandate the second thing is  that they're supposed to be full  employment they're supposed to pursue  that full employment and again  employment has gotten better over the  last few months but we still don't have  full employment and so you could again  argue that they're not doing a good job  with that mandate so there's plenty of  what i would consider healthy and  accurate critiques of the work that's  being done but i don't think that it is  fair to say that the federal reserve is  not focused on eliminating climate risk  or advancing racial and economic justice  because that is not their mandate it  would be like going into a high school  classroom in a math class and saying hey  you didn't answer any of the history  questions the kid would say yeah cause  i'm in math class right i'm focused on  math you're judging me on my math skills  you're not focused and judging me on my  history skills and so i think that we  have to be very very careful here that  we don't allow uh conversation  in social media from politicians et  cetera to start to actually shape  monetary policy right or to shape the  federal reserve and what i mean by that  is one of the key components of a  central bank that makes it effective is  that it is independent it actually is  not subject to the whims of the  political environment nor of individual  politicians that was true of the  republicans when president trump uh was  in office it's also true of these  politicians right it doesn't matter  which side of the aisle it doesn't  matter what their agenda is the central  bank should be independent of that  political environment and so i think  that this is uh pretty eye-opening in  terms of reminding ourselves what are  the mandates of the federal reserve they  don't include these things around  climate risk and racial economic justice  and so if we would like them to now have  that mandate we can go and try to add  that but i don't think that you can  actually ask for the non-reappointment  of chairman jerome powell  based on this criteria there's plenty of  other arguments you can make that i  think would be much better received than  making these hey guys bang bang